<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sambor Prei Kuk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Isanapura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sambor Prei Kuk is an archaeological site in Kampong Thom province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, identified with Isanapura, capital of the Chenla kingdom under King Isanavarman I in the 7th century. It contains over a hundred brick temples, many octagonal, decorated with carved lintels and reliefs. Their style prefigures the art of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, and the site was listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as a World Heritage Site in 2017.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sambor-prei-kuk.jpg" alt="Sambor Prei Kuk" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saxula · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sambor_Prei_Kuk-Map1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
